<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phetsarath Ratanavongsa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a>–<a href="/years/1959">1959</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Prince Phetsarath</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prince Phetsarath Ratanavongsa was a Lao nationalist leader and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-luang-phrabang">Kingdom of Luang Prabang</a>. After the Japanese surrender in 1945 he declared the unification and independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and led the Lao Issara movement, going into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> when the French returned in 1946. He came back to Laos in 1957 and is remembered as a father of Lao independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phetsarath-ratanavongsa.jpg" alt="Phetsarath Ratanavongsa" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prince_Phetsarath.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
